WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:06.930
Meyerhof reached biochemistry by an unlikely road.

2
00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:12.510
Bedridden for months by a kidney ailment in his 20s, he read poetry and wrote a

3
00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:17.510
doctoral thesis on the psychology of mental illness before deciding that life itself

4
00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:19.870
could be written in the language of physical law.

5
00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:26.733
His 1913 habilitation, "The Energetics of Living Cells," argued that a body is a

6
00:00:26.733 --> 00:00:32.173
thermodynamic system holding itself against entropy and he proved it with frog legs.

7
00:00:33.117 --> 00:00:38.297
Stimulating detached muscle to exhaustion without oxygen, he watched lactic acid

8
00:00:38.297 --> 00:00:43.637
pile up; when he readmitted air, only about a quarter of it burned for energy and

9
00:00:43.637 --> 00:00:45.517
the rest was rebuilt into glycogen.

10
00:00:46.017 --> 00:00:50.857
That recovery loop, the muscle repaying its "oxygen debt," won him a share of the

11
00:00:50.857 --> 00:00:52.677
1922 Nobel Prize.

12
00:00:53.617 --> 00:00:58.517
The theory was elegant, and he did not cling to it: when his assistant Karl Lohmann

13
00:00:58.517 --> 00:01:04.577
isolated ATP in 1929 and muscle was soon shown to contract with no lactic acid at

14
00:01:04.577 --> 00:01:08.657
all, Meyerhof retooled his whole laboratory around the new molecule.

15
00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:14.367
Fleeing France in 1940, found in a Marseille hotel lobby and asked what he needed,

16
00:01:14.827 --> 00:01:19.087
he asked not for food but for his notes and his library and carried both to

17
00:01:19.087 --> 00:01:19.527
Pennsylvania.
